## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical Kubernetes-native policy enforcement, CRD design, and live migration safety. It probes how storage policy changes ripple through PVCs and how to keep observability.

## Key Concepts
- CustomResourceDefinition scoped to Namespace
- Reconciliation loop and safe PVC migration
- StorageClass pinning and encryption key references
- Observability via CRD status, events, and metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-reconciler sketch
function reconcile(dataPolicy){
  for (pvc of allPVsInNamespace){
    if (!dataPolicy.allowedSC.includes(pvc.storageClass))
      migratePVC(pvc, dataPolicy.allowedSC[0]);
  }
  updateStatus(dataPolicy);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement safe rollback if migration fails?
- How would you audit policy changes across namespaces and clusters?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design admission-time governance patterns, CRD design, and distributed state handling in a multi-tenant cluster.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes admission webhooks and CRDs
- Distributed rate limiting (token bucket)
- Cross-replica consistency and RBAC
- Failure modes and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: CRD spec (JS-like for illustration)
class ApiRatePolicySpec {
  constructor(ns, maxRPS, burst) {
    this.namespace = ns
    this.maxRPS = maxRPS
    this.burst = burst
  }
}
```
```javascript
// Webhook skeleton (illustrative)
async function Admit(req) {
  const policy = lookupPolicy(req.namespace)
  if (!policy) return allow()
  const tokens = acquireToken(req.namespace, policy)
  return tokens ? allow() : deny("quota exceeded")
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test webhook scaling and Redis failover?
- What are the trade-offs of fail-closed vs fail-open during webhook outages?
